WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.000
Movie The Fibonacci Code

00:02.320 --> 00:10.000
Imagine a code so deeply woven into the fabric of nature that it appears everywhere from the spirals of galaxies to the curl of a seashell

00:10.240 --> 00:11.760
This isn't just any code

00:11.760 --> 00:18.120
It's a pattern a sequence that keeps reappearing almost as if nature herself is whispering a secret in our ears

00:18.480 --> 00:25.720
Take a look at a pine cone for instance ever noticed the mesmerizing arrangement of its bristles or think about a colony of rabbits

00:25.880 --> 00:28.560
Ever wondered how their population grows over time?

00:29.080 --> 00:34.400
Yes, there's a pattern there too, and it's not just them this mysterious sequence is all around us

00:34.720 --> 00:36.840
subtly shaping our world in ways

00:36.840 --> 00:41.580
We're only beginning to understand it's in the branching of trees the unfurling of ferns

00:41.720 --> 00:48.480
The arrangement of sunflower seeds and so much more this omnipresent pattern my friends is the Fibonacci sequence

00:48.760 --> 00:54.120
So what exactly is this elusive Fibonacci sequence that nature seems so fond of well?

00:54.120 --> 00:55.400
Let's break it down

00:55.400 --> 01:00.800
Imagine you have an empty basket and you decide to put an apple in it now you have one apple in your basket

01:00.840 --> 01:05.500
But one apple isn't enough so you add another now you have two apples in your basket

01:05.640 --> 01:09.700
Here's where things get interesting if you continue to add apples to your basket

01:09.700 --> 01:14.280
But each time you add the total number of apples you already have you'll begin to see a pattern

01:14.640 --> 01:21.800
You start with zero add one and you have one add another one and you have two add two to one and you have three

01:22.160 --> 01:28.520
This pattern of numbers 0 1 1 2 3 5 is the beginning of the Fibonacci sequence

01:28.640 --> 01:33.000
Each number in the sequence is simply the sum of the two numbers that came before it

01:33.560 --> 01:35.560
Now let's imagine you're climbing a staircase

01:36.280 --> 01:39.480
Each step you take is a number in the Fibonacci sequence

01:39.800 --> 01:41.800
First you take one step then another

01:42.240 --> 01:47.120
Next you take two steps at once then three then five this isn't just a fun math trick

01:47.120 --> 01:53.520
It's a pattern that appears throughout nature from the arrangement of seeds in a sunflower to the spiral of a nautilus shell and

01:53.880 --> 01:58.640
Just like that we have the Fibonacci sequence a simple series of numbers with profound implications

01:59.040 --> 02:06.320
But the magic of Fibonacci doesn't stop there. It leads us to something even more enchanting the golden ratio as

02:07.120 --> 02:12.120
We traverse further down the Fibonacci sequence. We discover a captivating pattern

02:12.600 --> 02:19.560
When we divide a number in the sequence by the one that immediately precedes it we notice a ratio emerging this ratio

02:19.960 --> 02:25.120
Approximately one point six one eight is known as the golden ratio. This isn't just any number

02:25.120 --> 02:30.260
It's a mathematical constant that has intrigued thinkers across disciplines for centuries

02:31.280 --> 02:39.240
mathematicians marvel at its unique properties artists and architects from the ancients to the moderns have used it to create works of breathtaking beauty as

02:39.480 --> 02:46.000
It's believed to yield the most aesthetically pleasing proportions this ratio derived from a simple sequence of numbers

02:46.240 --> 02:49.800
Has a universal allure it's found not only in art and architecture

02:50.360 --> 02:55.180
But also in nature from the spirals of galaxies to the patterns on a pine cone

02:56.000 --> 03:00.460
Incredible isn't it how a simple pattern of numbers can lead us to such a universally appealing ratio

03:00.720 --> 03:04.320
The Fibonacci sequence and the golden ratio are not just numbers

03:04.480 --> 03:11.520
They are a testament to the mathematical harmony that exists in our universe these concepts while seemingly complex

03:12.000 --> 03:16.400
Underline the simple elegant patterns found in nature art and architecture

03:17.720 --> 03:22.400
Their pervasive influence is a clear indication of the profound implications of this number pattern

03:22.440 --> 03:28.620
So the next time you marvel at a beautiful piece of art a magnificent architectural feat or even a humble pine cone

03:29.120 --> 03:31.680
Remember the Fibonacci sequence and the golden ratio

03:31.680 --> 03:35.000
They are the silent architects of beauty and harmony in our world

